{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_012ibnD8nUnRMFjGtR738o3i","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"The user wants me to generate detailed imagined experiment results for the \"worst\" tier scenario for each planned experiment in the experiment_plan. Let me analyze the worst tier definition and each planned experiment carefully.\n\nThe worst tier means:\n- exp-002 completes Phase 1 only (4 models × 3-4 benchmarks), Phases 2-4 blocked\n- Item_id% does not vary systematically across benchmarks (<10pp spread), OR gradient entirely explained by format confound\n- Some benchmarks show pathological variance structures\n- Model×item interaction magnitude is benchmark-specific but unpredictable\n\n\n- Convergence issues with Henderson I on some benchmarks\n- D-study budgets wildly different across benchmarks without interpretable pattern\n\nNow I'm going through each planned experiment to generate detailed worst-case predictions. exp-001 and exp-001b are already completed, so I need to focus on exp-002 through exp-008, with exp-008 having been merged into exp-002.\n\nFor exp-002, I'm expecting only Phase 1 to complete with 4 models across 3-4 benchmarks, no gradient data, and problematic variance structures on certain benchmarks. exp-003 is likely blocked entirely since it requires a different GPU type that hasn't been allocated yet. exp-004 depends on exp-001 (which is done) and exp-002 (partially done), so my analysis would be constrained to exp-001 data plus whatever Phase 1 of exp-002 produces—MMLU analysis would still work but cross-benchmark comparisons would be severely limited. exp-005 is cut off mid-description, so I need to continue evaluating the remaining experiments under this worst-case scenario. I'm reviewing the key metrics ranges for the worst-case tier—things like gradient spread staying minimal, model completion capped at 4-5, and the G-study items spread across a wide range without clear patterns. With Phase 1 of exp-002 done but Phase 2 uncertain, I can now map out predictions for each remaining experiment.\n\nGenerating detailed YAML output... I need to be thorough with result_rationale for each.\n\nLet me draft this carefully.\n\nFor experiments that are already DONE (exp-001, exp-001b), I should probably still include them but with their actual results, or skip them since they're done. The user says \"对 experiment_plan 中每个 planned 实验展开详细数据\" - so they want predictions for planned experiments. But exp-001 and exp-001b are DONE. exp-002 is RUNNING. The rest are planned.\n\nActually, looking more carefully: exp-001 status is DONE, exp-001b is DONE, exp-002 is RUNNING, exp-003 through exp-008 are planned. The user wants predictions for each, including the running one and planned ones.\n\nLet me think about what to include for each planned experiment:\n\n**exp-002**: Phase 1 completes with 4 models across 3-4 benchmarks like MMLU, ARC-Challenge, HellaSwag, and GSM8K, while Phase 2 (MATH) and Phase 3-4 (additional models) remain blocked. For the worst tier, I'm estimating MMLU around 42%, ARC-Challenge at 38%, HellaSwag at 45%, and GSM8K at 33%, with the ordering not following any clear task complexity pattern—HellaSwag performs best despite being a completion task, while GSM8K lags due to stochastic generation effects. I'm thinking through the variance structure and potential convergence issues—maybe ARC has minimal model-by-item interaction or GSM8K shows negative REML estimates for some components. Now I need to write out the full YAML predictions for all experiments. Since exp-001 and exp-001b are already done, I'll focus on predicting results for the remaining ones, structuring each with detailed rationales based on the benchmark characteristics and error patterns I've been analyzing.\n\nWriting YAML output... Looking at the experiment plan, I need to focus on the planned experiments—exp-003 through exp-008—and provide detailed predictions for each under the worst-case scenario, while noting that exp-001 and exp-001b are already complete with actual results and exp-002 is currently running. I'll structure this with specific numbers and rationales for how each experiment's scope changes, then work through the detailed data predictions for all seven experiments. exp-002 (Multi-benchmark, RUNNING)\n\nUnder worst tier: Phase 1 completes (4 original models × 4 benchmarks on westd-32570), Phases 2-4 blocked.\n\nBenchmarks completed: MMLU (replication from exp-001 data, or new runs), ARC-Challenge, HellaSwag, GSM8K.\nMATH blocked (Phase 2).\nAdditional models blocked (Phases 3-4).\n\nKey results under worst:\n- item_id%: MMLU ~42%, ARC ~37%, HellaSwag ~44%, GSM8K ~35%. No systematic ordering.\n- Gradient spread: ~9pp (35-44). Not monotonically ordered by any task taxonomy.\n- Permutation test p > 0.25 (not significant).\n- Model×item: MMLU ~34%, ARC ~22%, HellaSwag ~18%, GSM8K ~38%. Inconsistent across benchmarks.\n- GSM8K: residual >40% due to stochastic free-form generation. Pathological.\n- ARC: model×item negligible.\n- HellaSwag: near-zero precision component, negative REML estimate for precision×item.\n- G_item: MMLU 0.82, ARC 0.71, HellaSwag 0.78, GSM8K 0.52. No trend.\n- D-study items for G≥0.80: MMLU ~510, ARC ~350, HellaSwag ~280, GSM8K ~1100. Wildly different.\n- Within-format gradient: MC (MMLU vs ARC): 42% vs 37% = 5pp, but not in predicted direction. Free-form only GSM8K (no MATH to compare). Format confound analysis inconclusive but gradient NOT preserved within format.\n\nThe variance structure across benchmarks in the worst tier is highly idiosyncratic—each benchmark exhibits its own peculiar pattern of item effects, model interactions, and generalizability, with no coherent cross-benchmark trend emerging.\n\nexp-003 is completely blocked because I don't have access to a second GPU architecture; the infrastructure constraint limits me to the single RTX PRO 6000 setup.\n\nexp-004 proceeds with limitations using the MMLU data from exp-001, though the scope is narrower than originally planned.\n\nFor exp-004, the key findings show that bootstrap confidence intervals widen considerably when I only have 3-4 benchmarks to work with, and the variance structures diverge in problematic ways—REML and Henderson I estimates disagree on GSM8K but align on MMLU, D-study projections remain benchmark-specific without a generalizable formula, and IRT analysis only holds up reliably on MMLU while other benchmarks have too few conditions or unstable variance patterns.\n\nTemperature sensitivity varies dramatically across benchmarks: MMLU shifts less than 3 percentage points when treating temperature as fixed rather than random, but GSM8K shifts over 8 percentage points, indicating temperature dominates the decomposition there. Logprob scoring is only feasible on MMLU and ARC due to their multiple-choice formats, while HellaSwag and GSM8K don't have straightforward logprob access. The cross-benchmark design asymmetry is particularly problematic—MMLU's matched 2×2 design produces different decompositions than the other benchmarks' reduced designs, making direct comparisons unreliable.\n\nExp-005 with 14B models is blocked because it depends on exp-003 and requires GPUs with at least 48GB VRAM, and exp-006 on fine-tuning is similarly blocked.\n\nFor exp-006, the 1,728 fine-tuning runs demand substantial compute that would be reallocated away from exp-002 or other priorities, leaving results incomplete and unpublishable. If some runs do complete, they'd reveal that learning rate and seed dominate the fine-tuning variance structure—over 50% and 25% respectively—which differs fundamentally from the inference patterns, but incomplete data prevents drawing firm conclusions. Since exp-006 has no dependencies but competes for the same hardware, it gets deprioritized in the worst case when exp-002 needs the GPU time.\n\nExp-007 on LayerCast is deprioritized as a low-priority experiment that doesn't start because P0 experiments consume the entire GPU budget. Exp-008 on MATH is blocked since it's been merged into exp-002's Phase 2, which itself is blocked.\n\nNow I'm planning the comprehensive YAML with detailed metrics and rationales, and thinking through the tables and figures for each experiment—cross-benchmark variance decomposition tables and D-study curves for exp-002, bootstrap confidence interval tables for exp-004's MMLU components, and similar visualizations for the others.\n\nI need to structure this as a single YAML block with the tier, narrative guidance, and imagined experiments. For the completed experiments like exp-001 and exp-001b, I should include them with their actual results, though the user's request focuses on planned ones—but since exp-002 is running rather than planned, I'll include the full picture.\n\nWriting YAML structure...","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":0,"cache_read_input_tokens":30536,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":10,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"4b24b73a-f9a4-4c38-9191-0dbd0db6438e","uuid":"082f7a2a-d557-47f1-9ac4-dffffb735953"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_012ibnD8nUnRMFjGtR738o3i","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"```yaml\ntier: worst\nnarrative_guidance: >\n  Abandon cross-benchmark gradient as headline finding. The paper pivots to two axes:\n  (A) deep single-benchmark MMLU analysis (model×item quantification, D-study out-of-sample validation,\n  IRT comparison, full robustness package — all already strong from exp-001/exp-004), and\n  (B) 'benchmark-specific variance fingerprints' — each benchmark produces an idiosyncratic variance\n  structure that cannot be predicted from task taxonomy, format, or difficulty distribution alone.\n  The hook becomes: \"not only is there no universal reproducibility recipe — there is no universal\n  variance structure, and even task-type taxonomy fails to predict it.\" This is arguably more\n  surprising than a clean gradient. Claim 1 narrowed to MMLU with exploratory multi-benchmark\n  extension. Claim 3 strengthened — benchmark-specific budgets are the ONLY viable approach because\n  variance fingerprints are irreducibly benchmark-specific. Primary contribution shifts from gradient\n  discovery to: (i) model×item interaction quantification with D-study validation on MMLU,\n  (ii) IRT vs G-theory comparison, (iii) demonstration that variance structure is irreducibly\n  benchmark-specific. Same AAAI target but likely needs ACL ARR October cycle for rewriting.\n\nimagined_experiments:\n  - name: \"Multi-benchmark replication (Phase 1 only)\"\n    plan_id: \"exp-002\"\n    description: >\n      Phase 1 completes: 4 original models (Llama-3.1-8B, Gemma-2-9B, Mistral-7B, Qwen3-8B)\n      × 4 benchmarks (MMLU, ARC-Challenge, HellaSwag, GSM8K) × BF16 × 2 temps (0.0, 0.7)\n      × 6 prompts × 6 seeds × 4 orderings × 200 items each.\n      Phase 2 (MATH) blocked — dataset preprocessing issues with free-form answer extraction\n      pipeline; answer normalization for symbolic math expressions requires engineering work\n      beyond available bandwidth.\n      Phase 3 (Yi, OLMo) blocked — westd-15695 unavailable due to cluster maintenance or\n      allocation conflict.\n      Phase 4 (InternLM, DeepSeek) blocked — depends on Phase 3 infrastructure.\n    expected_results:\n      n_benchmarks_completed: 4\n      n_models_completed: 4\n      total_records_per_benchmark: 115200  # 4 models × 2 temps × 6 prompts × 6 seeds × 4 orderings × 200 items (but Gemma bf16-only = same here since design is bf16-only)\n      total_records_all: 460800\n      # --- Variance decomposition (item_id %) per benchmark ---\n      item_id_pct_MMLU: 41.8  # replicates exp-001 cross-model finding\n      item_id_pct_ARC: 37.2\n      item_id_pct_HellaSwag: 44.1\n      item_id_pct_GSM8K: 35.4\n      gradient_spread_item_id_pp: 8.7  # 44.1 - 35.4 = 8.7, under 10pp threshold\n      gradient_ordering: \"HellaSwag > MMLU > ARC > GSM8K\"\n      gradient_monotonicity_by_task_complexity: false  # HellaSwag (commonsense completion) > MMLU (knowledge MC) breaks any complexity ordering\n      gradient_permutation_test_p: 0.41  # not significant\n      # --- Model×item interaction per benchmark ---\n      model_x_item_pct_MMLU: 33.8\n      model_x_item_pct_ARC: 18.3\n      model_x_item_pct_HellaSwag: 15.7\n      model_x_item_pct_GSM8K: 38.9\n      model_x_item_range_pp: 23.2  # 38.9 - 15.7, inconsistent, no pattern\n      # --- Residual per benchmark ---\n      residual_pct_MMLU: 19.5\n      residual_pct_ARC: 28.4\n      residual_pct_HellaSwag: 25.3\n      residual_pct_GSM8K: 43.2  # pathological — stochastic generation dominates\n      # --- G_item (cross-model) per benchmark ---\n      G_item_MMLU: 0.821\n      G_item_ARC: 0.714\n      G_item_HellaSwag: 0.783\n      G_item_GSM8K: 0.518\n      G_item_ordering: \"MMLU > HellaSwag > ARC > GSM8K\"  # no monotonic trend with task type\n      # --- D-study items for G≥0.80 per benchmark ---\n      D_study_items_G80_MMLU: 510\n      D_study_items_G80_ARC: 340\n      D_study_items_G80_HellaSwag: 280\n      D_study_items_G80_GSM8K: 1150\n      D_study_range: \"280-1150 (4.1× spread)\"\n      # --- Convergence issues ---\n      Henderson_I_convergence_issues: \"GSM8K seed×item negative REML estimate (-0.3%), clamped to zero; HellaSwag precision×item near-zero (0.02%), REML estimate oscillates\"\n      # --- Within-format gradient ---\n      within_MC_gradient_MMLU_vs_ARC: \"42% vs 37% = 5pp, but ARC < MMLU contradicts complexity prediction (ARC is harder reasoning)\"\n      within_format_gradient_preserved: false\n      # --- Accuracy per benchmark (cross-model mean) ---\n      accuracy_MMLU: 0.681\n      accuracy_ARC: 0.592\n      accuracy_HellaSwag: 0.743\n      accuracy_GSM8K: 0.483\n      # --- Prompt sensitivity per benchmark ---\n      prompt_x_item_pct_MMLU: 4.2\n      prompt_x_item_pct_ARC: 6.8\n      prompt_x_item_pct_HellaSwag: 3.1\n      prompt_x_item_pct_GSM8K: 2.4  # low because residual absorbs everything\n    result_rationale: >\n      Phase 1 completion with Phase 2-4 blockage: MATH requires robust free-form answer\n      extraction (symbolic expressions, equivalent forms like \"1/2\" vs \"0.5\" vs \"\\\\frac{1}{2}\"),\n      which stalls on engineering work. Additional models blocked by hardware unavailability.\n\n      No systematic gradient: The variance structure is driven by benchmark-specific properties\n      that don't align with any single task taxonomy axis. HellaSwag shows highest item_id%\n      (44.1%) despite being \"commonsense completion\" — not the most complex task — because its\n      items have extremely stable difficulty rankings across models (4 candidates with clear\n      plausibility ordering). GSM8K shows lowest item_id% (35.4%) and highest residual (43.2%)\n      because free-form math generation introduces stochastic variation from decoding that\n      inflates the residual; the same model solving the same problem with the same prompt/seed\n      can produce different solution paths and occasionally different final answers at T>0.\n\n      Model×item interaction is inconsistent: GSM8K has the highest model×item (38.9%) because\n      models have strongly differentiated math capabilities — Qwen3-8B solves multi-step\n      problems that Mistral-7B cannot, and vice versa for word problems with unusual phrasing.\n      HellaSwag has lowest model×item (15.7%) because commonsense completion difficulty is more\n      universal across model families. This pattern (math > commonsense for model×item) is\n      interpretable post-hoc but was not predictable from task taxonomy alone.\n\n      ARC convergence: Henderson I produces near-zero estimates for several 3-way interactions\n      because the reduced design (2 temps × bf16-only) has limited degrees of freedom for\n      higher-order terms. REML on GSM8K produces a negative variance component for seed×item,\n      which is clamped to zero — seed choice has negligible systematic effect on item difficulty\n      in free-form generation because the dominant stochasticity is in the generation process\n      itself, not in CUDA-level seed effects.\n\n      D-study budgets span 4.1× (280-1150 items for G≥0.80), but this range has no interpretable\n      pattern: HellaSwag needs fewest items (high item_id%, low residual → efficient measurement)\n      while GSM8K needs most (low item_id%, high residual → measurement is noisy). This is\n      not a \"gradient\" — it's a benchmark-specific fingerprint determined by format, generation\n      stochasticity, and model-task interaction structure.\n\n      Within-format gradient fails: Within MC format, MMLU (42%) > ARC (37%), but ARC contains\n      harder science reasoning items — if a task-complexity gradient existed, ARC should be lower\n      OR higher than MMLU depending on the hypothesized direction. The 5pp difference is more\n      likely explained by item pool characteristics (ARC has fewer items with extreme difficulty,\n      compressing item_id%) than by task complexity. With only two MC benchmarks and no within-\n      format gradient, the format confound cannot be resolved or ruled out.\n    tables:\n      - name: \"Table: Cross-Benchmark Variance Decomposition (Phase 1, 4 models × 4 benchmarks)\"\n        description: >\n          Rows: variance components (item_id, model, model×item, prompt×item, seed×item,\n          temperature, temperature×item, residual). Columns: MMLU, ARC-Challenge, HellaSwag,\n          GSM8K. Shows no systematic gradient — component rankings differ across benchmarks.\n          GSM8K residual dominates (43.2%); HellaSwag item_id highest (44.1%); model×item\n          ranges from 15.7% (HellaSwag) to 38.9% (GSM8K) without pattern.\n      - name: \"Table: D-Study Budget Comparison Across Benchmarks\"\n        description: >\n          Items required for G≥0.70/0.80/0.90 per benchmark. Shows 4.1× spread in G≥0.80\n          requirements (280-1150 items). No interpretable pattern linking budget to task type.\n      - name: \"Table: Within-Format Gradient Test\"\n        description: >\n          MC format: MMLU (42%) vs ARC (37%), Δ=5pp. Free-form: GSM8K only (35.4%), no\n          comparator (MATH blocked). Completion: HellaSwag only (44.1%). Conclusion:\n          within-format gradient NOT preserved; insufficient data to disentangle format from\n          task-type effects.\n    figures_description:\n      - >\n        Stacked-bar variance decomposition across 4 benchmarks — the \"anti-gradient\" figure.\n        No systematic left-to-right ordering; bars show qualitatively different component\n        structures. GSM8K bar dominated by residual (gray, 43%); HellaSwag dominated by\n        item_id (blue, 44%); model×item (orange) varies 3× across benchmarks. Horizontal\n        dashed line at item_id=42% (MMLU reference) shows other benchmarks scatter above\n        and below without trend.\n      - >\n        D-study projection curves per benchmark (G vs item count, 4 panels or 4 lines on\n        one panel). Curves have wildly different slopes and asymptotes. GSM8K curve is\n        nearly flat — even 1000 items only reaches G≈0.75 because residual dominates.\n        HellaSwag curve rises steeply, crossing G=0.80 at ~280 items.\n    analysis: >\n      The cross-benchmark gradient — the planned headline finding — does not materialize.\n      Item_id% varies only 8.7pp across benchmarks (35-44%), with no monotonic ordering by\n      task complexity, format, or any other interpretable dimension. The permutation test for\n      monotonicity yields p=0.41 (null). Model×item interaction varies 23pp across benchmarks\n      but in an unpredictable pattern (highest on GSM8K, lowest on HellaSwag).\n\n      However, the \"benchmark-specific variance fingerprints\" finding is itself publishable.\n      The fact that variance structure is irreducibly benchmark-specific — not predictable\n      from task taxonomy — means (a) benchmark-specific G-studies are mandatory, not optional,\n      (b) reproducibility budgets cannot be transferred across benchmarks, and (c) conclusions\n      about \"which factors matter\" from single-benchmark studies do not generalize. This is\n      arguably more surprising and practically important than a clean gradient.\n\n      GSM8K's pathological residual (43.2%) is a standalone finding: free-form generation\n      introduces irreducible stochasticity that makes G-theory decomposition qualitatively\n      different from MC benchmarks. The seed×item negative REML estimate and convergence\n      oscillation on HellaSwag precision×item suggest that Henderson I (designed for balanced\n      continuous data) struggles with certain benchmark-specific variance structures,\n      strengthening the case for benchmark-specific methodology.\n\n      Phase 2-4 blockage limits the analysis: no MATH benchmark prevents within-free-form\n      gradient testing, and only 4 models prevents the planned expansion to 6-8 models that\n      would strengthen the model-as-facet exchangeability argument. The paper must explicitly\n      scope to \"4 models × 4 benchmarks, exploratory Phase 1 analysis.\"\n\n  - name: \"Multi-hardware G-study\"\n    plan_id: \"exp-003\"\n    description: >\n      Completely blocked. No second GPU type becomes available during the experimental window.\n      Hardware claim remains scoped to \"numerical precision on RTX PRO 6000 (Blackwell).\"\n    expected_results:\n      status: blocked\n      n_gpu_types: 1\n      data_collected: 0\n      hardware_crossing_completed: false\n      reason: \"No alternative GPU allocation (A100/H100/AMD) secured\"\n    result_rationale: >\n      The experiment requires a fundamentally different GPU architecture to test hardware×methodology\n      interaction. In the worst tier, the compute allocation is fully consumed by exp-002 Phase 1\n      on the single available machine (westd-32570). The planned second machine (westd-15695) is\n      either unavailable, has the same GPU type, or has allocation conflicts. Without a second GPU\n      type, the entire experiment is infeasible.\n\n      Impact on paper: Claim 2 must be scoped to \"precision separability on a single GPU\n      architecture\" rather than \"infrastructure separability.\" This is already the planned\n      defensive scoping per D003/D007. The paper acknowledges hardware crossing as future work\n      and cites Yuan et al. (2025) for evidence that hardware effects exist but are unquantified\n      in a G-theory framework. This weakens but does not kill Claim 2 — precision separability\n      under binary scoring is still a valid confirmatory finding.\n    tables: []\n    figures_description: []\n    analysis: >\n      No analysis possible. Experiment contributes zero data. Paper must explicitly state\n      \"hardware limited to a single GPU architecture (NVIDIA RTX PRO 6000, Blackwell)\" in\n      Methods and Limitations. The separability claim (Claim 2) survives but is narrowly\n      scoped — \"precision × methodology separability\" rather than \"infrastructure × methodology\n      separability.\" Reviewer concern #1 (\"Only N GPU types\") is unaddressed and must be\n      handled as a stated limitation with future work framing.\n\n  - name: \"Comprehensive analysis\"\n    plan_id: \"exp-004\"\n    description: >\n      Analysis proceeds on available data: exp-001 MMLU (345,600 records, 4 models, full\n      design) + exp-002 Phase 1 (460,800 records, 4 models × 4 benchmarks, reduced design).\n      Most sub-analyses (a)-(q) are feasible on MMLU. Cross-benchmark sub-analyses are\n      limited by exp-002's reduced scope and pathological variance structures.\n    expected_results:\n      # --- (a) Model-as-facet reanalysis ---\n      model_as_facet_MMLU_item_id_pct: 42.1  # already validated in exp-001\n      model_as_facet_MMLU_model_x_item_pct: 33.6\n      model_as_facet_other_benchmarks: \"attempted but unreliable on GSM8K (residual >40% absorbs signal)\"\n      # --- (b) Bootstrap CIs ---\n      bootstrap_n_resamples: 1000\n      bootstrap_CI_item_id_MMLU: [36.0, 48.8]  # 95% CI\n      bootstrap_CI_model_x_item_MMLU: [28.4, 37.9]\n      bootstrap_CI_width_item_id_MMLU_pp: 12.8\n      bootstrap_CI_cross_benchmark: \"wide — ARC item_id CI [28, 47], GSM8K item_id CI [24, 48]\"\n      bootstrap_CI_GSM8K_residual: [35.1, 52.8]  # wide due to stochastic generation\n      # --- (c) D-study projections with CIs ---\n      D_study_MMLU_items_G80_CI: [430, 610]  # point estimate 510\n      D_study_MMLU_items_G90_CI: [1050, 1680]\n      D_study_cross_benchmark_CIs: \"too wide for practical guidance on GSM8K (G80: [600, 2100])\"\n      # --- (d) REML vs Henderson I ---\n      REML_Henderson_agreement_MMLU: \"all components within <0.5pp\"\n      REML_Henderson_agreement_ARC: \"within <1.2pp for top-3 components\"\n      REML_Henderson_agreement_GSM8K: \"diverge — Henderson I seed×item=1.8%, REML=-0.3% (clamped to 0)\"\n      REML_Henderson_agreement_HellaSwag: \"within <0.8pp except precision×item (Henderson 0.02%, REML oscillates 0.00-0.15%)\"\n      # --- (e) Logprob scoring ---\n      logprob_analysis_feasible: \"MMLU and ARC only (MC format with logprob access)\"\n      logprob_item_id_pct_MMLU: 55.3  # higher on continuous scale\n      logprob_model_x_item_pct_MMLU: 28.1\n      logprob_residual_pct_MMLU: 11.2  # lower residual on continuous scale\n      logprob_HellaSwag: \"feasible in principle (completion scoring) but not implemented\"\n      logprob_GSM8K: \"not feasible — free-form generation has no natural logprob score\"\n      # --- (f) Ranking stability ---\n      model_ranking_stable_across_precision_MMLU: true\n      model_ranking_stable_across_precision_GSM8K: false  # Mistral and Qwen swap at T=0.7\n      # --- (g) Higher-order interactions ---\n      3way_interactions_total_MMLU_pct: 4.2\n      3way_interactions_total_ARC_pct: 6.8\n      3way_interactions_total_GSM8K_pct: 2.1  # small because residual absorbs everything\n      residual_after_3way_MMLU_pct: 15.3\n      # --- (h) IRT vs G-theory comparison ---\n      IRT_items_G80_MMLU: 116\n      Gtheory_items_G80_MMLU: 510\n      IRT_to_Gtheory_ratio: 4.4\n      IRT_comparison_feasible_on: \"MMLU only — other benchmarks lack sufficient data or have pathological structures\"\n      # --- (i) Power analysis ---\n      power_to_detect_5pct_interaction_MMLU: 0.99\n      power_to_detect_5pct_interaction_ARC: 0.94\n      power_to_detect_5pct_interaction_GSM8K: 0.78  # reduced by high residual\n      # --- (k) Fixed vs random sensitivity ---\n      fixed_vs_random_delta_MMLU_pp: 2.8  # <5%, robust\n      fixed_vs_random_delta_GSM8K_pp: 8.4  # >5%, NOT robust — temperature dominates GSM8K\n      # --- (l) Binary data limitation ---\n      bernoulli_p1p_correlation_with_item_var_MMLU: 0.31  # moderate\n      bernoulli_p1p_correlation_with_item_var_GSM8K: 0.68  # high — low accuracy items have compressed variance\n      # --- (m) Bootstrap vs jackknife ---\n      bootstrap_jackknife_CI_ratio_MMLU: 0.98  # excellent agreement\n      bootstrap_jackknife_CI_ratio_ARC: 1.03\n      bootstrap_jackknife_CI_ratio_GSM8K: 1.14  # jackknife wider, suggesting bootstrap anti-conservative\n      # --- (n) Design asymmetry ---\n      MMLU_full_vs_matched_2x2_delta_item_id_pp: 2.9  # <5%, robust\n      MMLU_full_vs_matched_2x2_delta_model_x_item_pp: 1.4\n      # --- (o) G at full benchmark size ---\n      G_at_14K_items_MMLU_projection: 0.987  # full MMLU is highly reliable\n      G_at_full_ARC_2590_items_projection: 0.953\n      G_at_full_GSM8K_1319_items_projection: 0.812  # barely crosses 0.80 even at full size\n      # --- (p) Logprob across benchmarks ---\n      logprob_cross_benchmark: \"only MMLU and ARC feasible, insufficient for cross-benchmark claims\"\n      # --- (q) GPU cost analysis ---\n      compute_cost_per_G80_MMLU: \"~$45 (510 items × 4 models × 6 prompts × 6 seeds)\"\n      compute_cost_per_G80_GSM8K: \"~$210 (1150 items × 4 models × 6 prompts × 6 seeds, longer generation)\"\n    result_rationale: >\n      MMLU analyses are strong and largely replicate exp-001 findings with bootstrap CIs\n      and additional robustness checks. The IRT vs G-theory comparison (4.4× item ratio)\n      is clean on MMLU and becomes a primary contribution.\n\n      Cross-benchmark analyses are degraded by three compounding factors:\n      (1) Only 4 benchmarks (no MATH) with only 4 models — limited statistical power for\n      cross-benchmark generalizations and wide bootstrap CIs.\n      (2) GSM8K's pathological variance structure (residual >40%, negative REML estimates)\n      makes several analyses unreliable or uninterpretable on that benchmark. The high\n      residual means G-theory components are estimated with low precision, widening CIs\n      and making D-study projections impractical (CI for G80 items: [600, 2100]).\n      (3) Fixed-vs-random sensitivity analysis fails on GSM8K (Δ>5pp), meaning the\n      methodological choice of treating temperature as random vs fixed materially affects\n      results — another benchmark-specific pathology.\n\n      REML diverges from Henderson I on GSM8K because the balanced design assumption is\n      stressed by near-zero or negative components. Henderson I is unbiased for balanced\n      designs but produces interpretable results even when components are near-zero; REML\n      tries to find ML estimates and can produce negative values that must be clamped.\n      This divergence is itself a finding: Henderson I is reliable for well-behaved MC\n      benchmarks but may be problematic for free-form generation benchmarks.\n\n      The logprob analysis cannot extend beyond MC formats (MMLU, ARC) because free-form\n      and completion tasks lack a natural single-token probability score. This means the\n      \"Evaluation Metrics\" claim in the title is only partially supported, and the title\n      may need adjustment per MD9.\n\n      Bootstrap vs jackknife agreement is good on MMLU/ARC (ratio 0.98-1.03) but marginal\n      on GSM8K (ratio 1.14), suggesting bootstrap CIs on GSM8K are slightly anti-conservative\n      due to non-independence in the stochastic generation process.\n    tables:\n      - name: \"Table: Bootstrap 95% CIs for All Variance Components (MMLU, 4 models)\"\n        description: >\n          7 variance components × point estimate + 95% CI. item_id: 42.1% [36.0, 48.8],\n          model×item: 33.6% [28.4, 37.9], residual: 19.5% [16.2, 23.1]. All CIs exclude\n          zero for top-4 components. prompt×item and seed×item CIs overlap.\n      - name: \"Table: REML vs Henderson I Comparison Across Benchmarks\"\n        description: >\n          4 benchmarks × 7 components × Henderson I estimate + REML estimate + Δ.\n          MMLU: all Δ < 0.5pp. ARC: Δ < 1.2pp. HellaSwag: precision×item oscillates.\n          GSM8K: seed×item diverges (1.8% vs clamped 0%). Conclusion: Henderson I reliable\n          for MC benchmarks; caution needed for free-form generation.\n      - name: \"Table: IRT vs G-Theory Item Requirements (MMLU)\"\n        description: >\n          Reliability threshold × IRT items × G-theory items × ratio. G≥0.70: 55 vs 210\n          (3.8×). G≥0.80: 116 vs 510 (4.4×). G≥0.90: 280 vs 1350 (4.8×). Decomposition\n          of G-theory excess: prompt×item accounts for 22%, seed×item 23%, residual 55%\n          of additional items.\n      - name: \"Table: Fixed vs Random Sensitivity Analysis\"\n        description: >\n          4 benchmarks × top-4 components × random estimate + fixed estimate + Δ.\n          MMLU Δ < 3pp (robust). GSM8K Δ = 8.4pp for item_id (NOT robust — temperature\n          reclassification redistributes 8pp from temperature to residual).\n      - name: \"Table: D-Study Budget Comparison with Bootstrap CIs\"\n        description: >\n          4 benchmarks × items for G≥0.80 [95% CI]. MMLU: 510 [430, 610]. ARC: 340 [260, 470].\n          HellaSwag: 280 [210, 380]. GSM8K: 1150 [600, 2100]. The GSM8K CI spans 3.5×,\n          making the point estimate practically meaningless.\n    figures_description:\n      - >\n        Forest plot of variance component CIs across 4 benchmarks. Each benchmark is a\n        panel; within each panel, components are rows with point estimates (dots) and 95%\n        CI whiskers. Visually shows that CI widths vary dramatically across benchmarks\n        (tight on MMLU, wide on GSM8K) and component rankings are inconsistent.\n      - >\n        IRT vs G-theory comparison bar chart (MMLU only). Two bars per reliability threshold\n        (0.70, 0.80, 0.90). G-theory bar decomposed into sub-segments showing which\n        facets (prompt×item, seed×item, residual) account for the extra items beyond IRT.\n        Clear visual showing the \"multi-facet reliability cost.\"\n      - >\n        D-study projection curves with 95% CI ribbons for 4 benchmarks (overlaid on single\n        panel). MMLU and HellaSwag curves rise steeply with narrow ribbons. GSM8K curve\n        is nearly flat with a wide ribbon that barely touches G=0.80 at n=1150.\n    analysis: >\n      exp-004 is the analytical core of the paper under worst tier. The MMLU-specific\n      analyses are strong: bootstrap CIs confirm top-3 component ordering, IRT comparison\n      produces the clean 4.4× ratio, REML validates Henderson I, and the binary data\n      robustness checks all pass. These form the backbone of the paper.\n\n      Cross-benchmark analyses reveal the \"irreducibly benchmark-specific\" finding but\n      cannot support strong quantitative claims due to wide CIs and benchmark-specific\n      pathologies. GSM8K is particularly problematic: high residual → wide CIs → unreliable\n      D-study projections → fixed-vs-random sensitivity failure. The paper should present\n      GSM8K results as \"illustrative of pathological variance structure in free-form\n      generation\" rather than as reliable G-theory estimates.\n\n      The IRT vs G-theory comparison is limited to MMLU (the only benchmark with full\n      design + clean variance structure + IRT comparability). This is sufficient for a\n      strong numbered contribution but prevents claiming \"cross-benchmark IRT comparison.\"\n\n      Key paper structure implication: MMLU analysis (exp-001 + exp-004 MMLU-specific)\n      occupies 60-70% of the results section. Cross-benchmark analysis (exp-002 + exp-004\n      cross-benchmark) occupies 30-40% as an \"exploratory extension\" with explicit\n      caveats about reduced design, limited benchmarks, and pathological structures.\n\n  - name: \"14B model extension\"\n    plan_id: \"exp-005\"\n    description: >\n      Blocked. Depends on exp-003 (hardware) which is blocked, and requires ≥48GB VRAM\n      GPUs not currently allocated. No 14B model data is collected.\n    expected_results:\n      status: blocked\n      n_14B_models_tested: 0\n      scale_analysis_completed: false\n      reason: \"Depends on exp-003 (blocked); requires high-VRAM GPU not available\"\n    result_rationale: >\n      exp-005 has two blocking dependencies: (1) it was designed to run on datacenter GPUs\n      at BF16/FP16 after exp-003 establishes the multi-hardware baseline, and (2) 14B models\n      require ≥48GB VRAM for comfortable inference with vLLM, which is available on the\n      existing RTX PRO 6000 (96GB) but the GPU budget is fully consumed by exp-002 Phase 1.\n      Even if the hardware dependency were relaxed, the compute hours (100h) compete directly\n      with exp-002's needs. Under worst-tier resource constraints, P1 experiments are\n      systematically deprioritized in favor of P0 experiments.\n\n      Impact on paper: The \"model scale\" dimension is entirely absent. The paper must state\n      \"all models are 7-9B parameter scale\" as a limitation. This is defensible — the paper\n      studies variance structure, not scaling laws — but reviewers may flag it. The planned\n      comparison between dense (Qwen3-14B) and reasoning-distilled (DeepSeek-R1-Distill-14B)\n      architectures is lost. This comparison would have been interesting for the model×item\n      interaction analysis (does distillation change the interaction pattern?) but is not\n      essential for the core claims.\n    tables: []\n    figures_description: []\n    analysis: >\n      No analysis possible. Limitation statement: \"Our analysis is limited to 7-9B parameter\n      open-weight models. Whether variance structure changes with model scale is an important\n      open question — larger models may show reduced model×item interaction if they converge\n      toward similar capability profiles, or increased interaction if scale enables more\n      specialized item-level behaviors.\"\n\n  - name: \"Fine-tuning G-study\"\n    plan_id: \"exp-006\"\n    description: >\n      Blocked by compute reallocation. The 1,728 fine-tuning runs (432 per task × 4 tasks)\n      require ~200 GPU hours on westd-32570, which is fully allocated to exp-002 Phase 1\n      and exp-001 post-processing. Even partial completion (1 task) would consume 50+ hours.\n    expected_results:\n      status: blocked\n      n_finetuning_runs_completed: 0\n      tasks_completed: 0\n      reason: \"GPU budget fully consumed by P0 experiments (exp-002 Phase 1)\"\n    result_rationale: >\n      exp-006 is independent (no experimental dependencies) but competes for the same GPU\n      resource. Under worst-tier constraints, exp-002 Phase 1 alone consumes 200-250 GPU\n      hours (4 models × 4 benchmarks × ~15h per model-benchmark combination with the full\n      prompt/seed/ordering crossing). With exp-001 post-processing reruns and validation\n      checks, the total GPU budget is exhausted before exp-006 can start.\n\n      Even if exp-006 were partially completed (e.g., SST-2 only), the results would be\n      insufficient for even a Discussion-section preliminary observation — a single\n      fine-tuning task cannot support variance structure comparison between inference and\n      fine-tuning paradigms.\n\n      Impact on paper: Fine-tuning analysis is mentioned as future work in Discussion. The\n      paper explicitly qualifies: \"Our analysis addresses inference-time evaluation only.\n      Fine-tuning variance structure — where learning rate, data order, and seed interact\n      with task-specific loss landscapes — is a qualitatively different problem that we\n      leave for future work.\" Per D006 Dream #3 directive, fine-tuning was already downgraded\n      to Discussion-section preliminary observation, so blockage has minimal impact on paper\n      structure.\n    tables: []\n    figures_description: []\n    analysis: >\n      No analysis possible. The fine-tuning extension is entirely absent from the paper.\n      Per existing framing directives, this was already downgraded to Discussion section.\n      Cite Bui et al. (2025) for evidence that fine-tuning variance structure differs\n      substantially (RTES variance=18.22 for RoBERTa on COPA) and position as motivated\n      future work.\n\n  - name: \"LayerCast precision study\"\n    plan_id: \"exp-007\"\n    description: >\n      Deprioritized (P2). Not started due to GPU budget exhaustion from P0 experiments.\n      LayerCast (FP16 storage + FP32 compute) as a variance mitigation strategy is not\n      tested.\n    expected_results:\n      status: blocked\n      layercast_tested: false\n      variance_reduction_quantified: false\n      reason: \"P2 priority; GPU budget exhausted by P0 experiments\"\n    result_rationale: >\n      exp-007 is the lowest-priority experiment (P2) and depends on exp-001 being complete\n      (it is). However, the 30 GPU hours required compete with exp-002 Phase 1 needs. Under\n      worst-tier constraints, all discretionary GPU budget is consumed by the P0 pipeline.\n      Additionally, the scientific value of LayerCast testing is reduced once BF16-vs-FP16\n      sensitivity analysis (already completed in exp-001) shows BF16≈FP16 (Δ=0.23pp) — if\n      standard precision choices don't matter, testing a mixed-precision strategy is low\n      priority.\n\n      Impact on paper: No variance mitigation strategy is tested. The paper's D-study\n      budgets address \"how many items/prompts/seeds\" but not \"how to reduce precision\n      variance.\" This is consistent with the finding that precision contributes <0.1%\n      variance under binary scoring — there is nothing to mitigate. The paper can note:\n      \"LayerCast and other mixed-precision strategies may be relevant for text-exact-match\n      scoring where precision×temperature interaction is detectable (1.3%).\"\n    tables: []\n    figures_description: []\n    analysis: >\n      No analysis possible. Minimal impact on paper — precision is already shown to be\n      negligible under binary scoring. LayerCast is mentioned as a potential mitigation\n      for text-exact-match scoring in the Discussion section.\n\n  - name: \"MATH benchmark (merged into exp-002 Phase 2)\"\n    plan_id: \"exp-008\"\n    description: >\n      Blocked. MATH was merged into exp-002 as the 5th benchmark in Phase 2, which is\n      blocked by answer extraction pipeline issues. Free-form symbolic math answer\n      normalization (handling equivalent forms: \"1/2\", \"0.5\", \"\\\\frac{1}{2}\", \"0.500\")\n      requires engineering work that stalls.\n    expected_results:\n      status: blocked\n      math_items_collected: 0\n      answer_extraction_pipeline_status: \"incomplete — symbolic normalization handles ~70% of MATH answers correctly, remaining 30% require regex/sympy extensions\"\n      reason: \"Merged into exp-002 Phase 2; blocked by answer normalization engineering\"\n    result_rationale: >\n      MATH benchmark presents unique data engineering challenges compared to MC benchmarks\n      (MMLU, ARC, HellaSwag) and even compared to GSM8K (which has simpler numerical\n      answers). MATH answers include symbolic expressions, fractions, roots, and multiple\n      equivalent forms that require robust normalization. The extraction pipeline handles\n      simple numerical answers (GSM8K-style) but fails on ~30% of MATH items where the\n      answer is symbolic. Building a robust sympy-based normalization pipeline is estimated\n      at 20-30 engineering hours — work that is deprioritized under worst-tier constraints\n      where the focus is on completing Phase 1 analysis and writing.\n\n      Impact on paper: The absence of MATH critically undermines the within-format gradient\n      test. With only GSM8K as a free-form benchmark, the within-format gradient analysis\n      has no free-form comparator. The paper cannot distinguish whether GSM8K's low item_id%\n      (35.4%) is due to free-form format, mathematical reasoning task type, or\n      stochastic generation. This ambiguity is a significant limitation for the\n      \"benchmark-specific fingerprints\" framing — without MATH, the paper cannot determine\n      whether the fingerprint is driven by format or task content within the free-form category.\n\n      The paper addresses this by: (a) acknowledging the limitation explicitly, (b) noting\n      that MATH's absence prevents within-free-form gradient testing, and (c) framing MATH\n      as the highest-priority extension for future work. The \"benchmark-specific fingerprints\"\n      claim is still supported by the MC benchmarks (MMLU vs ARC have different structures\n      despite same format) and the cross-format comparison (MC vs completion vs free-form),\n      but the within-format argument is weaker.\n    tables: []\n    figures_description: []\n    analysis: >\n      No analysis possible. The paper's cross-benchmark analysis is limited to 4 benchmarks\n      (MMLU, ARC, HellaSwag, GSM8K) spanning 3 formats (MC, completion, free-form) but\n      with only 1-2 benchmarks per format. Within-format gradient testing is limited to\n      MC (MMLU vs ARC = 2 data points). The \"benchmark-specific fingerprints\" finding\n      survives but is less compelling without a within-format comparator for free-form tasks.\n```"}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":0,"cache_read_input_tokens":30536,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":10,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"4b24b73a-f9a4-4c38-9191-0dbd0db6438e","uuid":"eb605ecc-c4a5-4873-ae58-cd3e5bb8508b"}
{"type":"result","subtype":"success","is_error":false,"api_error_status":null,"duration_ms":322542,"duration_api_ms":323897,"num_turns":1,"result":"```yaml\ntier: worst\nnarrative_guidance: >\n  Abandon cross-benchmark gradient as headline finding. The paper pivots to two axes:\n  (A) deep single-benchmark MMLU analysis (model×item quantification, D-study out-of-sample validation,\n  IRT comparison, full robustness package — all already strong from exp-001/exp-004), and\n  (B) 'benchmark-specific variance fingerprints' — each benchmark produces an idiosyncratic variance\n  structure that cannot be predicted from task taxonomy, format, or difficulty distribution alone.\n  The hook becomes: \"not only is there no universal reproducibility recipe — there is no universal\n  variance structure, and even task-type taxonomy fails to predict it.\" This is arguably more\n  surprising than a clean gradient. Claim 1 narrowed to MMLU with exploratory multi-benchmark\n  extension. Claim 3 strengthened — benchmark-specific budgets are the ONLY viable approach because\n  variance fingerprints are irreducibly benchmark-specific. Primary contribution shifts from gradient\n  discovery to: (i) model×item interaction quantification with D-study validation on MMLU,\n  (ii) IRT vs G-theory comparison, (iii) demonstration that variance structure is irreducibly\n  benchmark-specific. Same AAAI target but likely needs ACL ARR October cycle for rewriting.\n\nimagined_experiments:\n  - name: \"Multi-benchmark replication (Phase 1 only)\"\n    plan_id: \"exp-002\"\n    description: >\n      Phase 1 completes: 4 original models (Llama-3.1-8B, Gemma-2-9B, Mistral-7B, Qwen3-8B)\n      × 4 benchmarks (MMLU, ARC-Challenge, HellaSwag, GSM8K) × BF16 × 2 temps (0.0, 0.7)\n      × 6 prompts × 6 seeds × 4 orderings × 200 items each.\n      Phase 2 (MATH) blocked — dataset preprocessing issues with free-form answer extraction\n      pipeline; answer normalization for symbolic math expressions requires engineering work\n      beyond available bandwidth.\n      Phase 3 (Yi, OLMo) blocked — westd-15695 unavailable due to cluster maintenance or\n      allocation conflict.\n      Phase 4 (InternLM, DeepSeek) blocked — depends on Phase 3 infrastructure.\n    expected_results:\n      n_benchmarks_completed: 4\n      n_models_completed: 4\n      total_records_per_benchmark: 115200  # 4 models × 2 temps × 6 prompts × 6 seeds × 4 orderings × 200 items (but Gemma bf16-only = same here since design is bf16-only)\n      total_records_all: 460800\n      # --- Variance decomposition (item_id %) per benchmark ---\n      item_id_pct_MMLU: 41.8  # replicates exp-001 cross-model finding\n      item_id_pct_ARC: 37.2\n      item_id_pct_HellaSwag: 44.1\n      item_id_pct_GSM8K: 35.4\n      gradient_spread_item_id_pp: 8.7  # 44.1 - 35.4 = 8.7, under 10pp threshold\n      gradient_ordering: \"HellaSwag > MMLU > ARC > GSM8K\"\n      gradient_monotonicity_by_task_complexity: false  # HellaSwag (commonsense completion) > MMLU (knowledge MC) breaks any complexity ordering\n      gradient_permutation_test_p: 0.41  # not significant\n      # --- Model×item interaction per benchmark ---\n      model_x_item_pct_MMLU: 33.8\n      model_x_item_pct_ARC: 18.3\n      model_x_item_pct_HellaSwag: 15.7\n      model_x_item_pct_GSM8K: 38.9\n      model_x_item_range_pp: 23.2  # 38.9 - 15.7, inconsistent, no pattern\n      # --- Residual per benchmark ---\n      residual_pct_MMLU: 19.5\n      residual_pct_ARC: 28.4\n      residual_pct_HellaSwag: 25.3\n      residual_pct_GSM8K: 43.2  # pathological — stochastic generation dominates\n      # --- G_item (cross-model) per benchmark ---\n      G_item_MMLU: 0.821\n      G_item_ARC: 0.714\n      G_item_HellaSwag: 0.783\n      G_item_GSM8K: 0.518\n      G_item_ordering: \"MMLU > HellaSwag > ARC > GSM8K\"  # no monotonic trend with task type\n      # --- D-study items for G≥0.80 per benchmark ---\n      D_study_items_G80_MMLU: 510\n      D_study_items_G80_ARC: 340\n      D_study_items_G80_HellaSwag: 280\n      D_study_items_G80_GSM8K: 1150\n      D_study_range: \"280-1150 (4.1× spread)\"\n      # --- Convergence issues ---\n      Henderson_I_convergence_issues: \"GSM8K seed×item negative REML estimate (-0.3%), clamped to zero; HellaSwag precision×item near-zero (0.02%), REML estimate oscillates\"\n      # --- Within-format gradient ---\n      within_MC_gradient_MMLU_vs_ARC: \"42% vs 37% = 5pp, but ARC < MMLU contradicts complexity prediction (ARC is harder reasoning)\"\n      within_format_gradient_preserved: false\n      # --- Accuracy per benchmark (cross-model mean) ---\n      accuracy_MMLU: 0.681\n      accuracy_ARC: 0.592\n      accuracy_HellaSwag: 0.743\n      accuracy_GSM8K: 0.483\n      # --- Prompt sensitivity per benchmark ---\n      prompt_x_item_pct_MMLU: 4.2\n      prompt_x_item_pct_ARC: 6.8\n      prompt_x_item_pct_HellaSwag: 3.1\n      prompt_x_item_pct_GSM8K: 2.4  # low because residual absorbs everything\n    result_rationale: >\n      Phase 1 completion with Phase 2-4 blockage: MATH requires robust free-form answer\n      extraction (symbolic expressions, equivalent forms like \"1/2\" vs \"0.5\" vs \"\\\\frac{1}{2}\"),\n      which stalls on engineering work. Additional models blocked by hardware unavailability.\n\n      No systematic gradient: The variance structure is driven by benchmark-specific properties\n      that don't align with any single task taxonomy axis. HellaSwag shows highest item_id%\n      (44.1%) despite being \"commonsense completion\" — not the most complex task — because its\n      items have extremely stable difficulty rankings across models (4 candidates with clear\n      plausibility ordering). GSM8K shows lowest item_id% (35.4%) and highest residual (43.2%)\n      because free-form math generation introduces stochastic variation from decoding that\n      inflates the residual; the same model solving the same problem with the same prompt/seed\n      can produce different solution paths and occasionally different final answers at T>0.\n\n      Model×item interaction is inconsistent: GSM8K has the highest model×item (38.9%) because\n      models have strongly differentiated math capabilities — Qwen3-8B solves multi-step\n      problems that Mistral-7B cannot, and vice versa for word problems with unusual phrasing.\n      HellaSwag has lowest model×item (15.7%) because commonsense completion difficulty is more\n      universal across model families. This pattern (math > commonsense for model×item) is\n      interpretable post-hoc but was not predictable from task taxonomy alone.\n\n      ARC convergence: Henderson I produces near-zero estimates for several 3-way interactions\n      because the reduced design (2 temps × bf16-only) has limited degrees of freedom for\n      higher-order terms. REML on GSM8K produces a negative variance component for seed×item,\n      which is clamped to zero — seed choice has negligible systematic effect on item difficulty\n      in free-form generation because the dominant stochasticity is in the generation process\n      itself, not in CUDA-level seed effects.\n\n      D-study budgets span 4.1× (280-1150 items for G≥0.80), but this range has no interpretable\n      pattern: HellaSwag needs fewest items (high item_id%, low residual → efficient measurement)\n      while GSM8K needs most (low item_id%, high residual → measurement is noisy). This is\n      not a \"gradient\" — it's a benchmark-specific fingerprint determined by format, generation\n      stochasticity, and model-task interaction structure.\n\n      Within-format gradient fails: Within MC format, MMLU (42%) > ARC (37%), but ARC contains\n      harder science reasoning items — if a task-complexity gradient existed, ARC should be lower\n      OR higher than MMLU depending on the hypothesized direction. The 5pp difference is more\n      likely explained by item pool characteristics (ARC has fewer items with extreme difficulty,\n      compressing item_id%) than by task complexity. With only two MC benchmarks and no within-\n      format gradient, the format confound cannot be resolved or ruled out.\n    tables:\n      - name: \"Table: Cross-Benchmark Variance Decomposition (Phase 1, 4 models × 4 benchmarks)\"\n        description: >\n          Rows: variance components (item_id, model, model×item, prompt×item, seed×item,\n          temperature, temperature×item, residual). Columns: MMLU, ARC-Challenge, HellaSwag,\n          GSM8K. Shows no systematic gradient — component rankings differ across benchmarks.\n          GSM8K residual dominates (43.2%); HellaSwag item_id highest (44.1%); model×item\n          ranges from 15.7% (HellaSwag) to 38.9% (GSM8K) without pattern.\n      - name: \"Table: D-Study Budget Comparison Across Benchmarks\"\n        description: >\n          Items required for G≥0.70/0.80/0.90 per benchmark. Shows 4.1× spread in G≥0.80\n          requirements (280-1150 items). No interpretable pattern linking budget to task type.\n      - name: \"Table: Within-Format Gradient Test\"\n        description: >\n          MC format: MMLU (42%) vs ARC (37%), Δ=5pp. Free-form: GSM8K only (35.4%), no\n          comparator (MATH blocked). Completion: HellaSwag only (44.1%). Conclusion:\n          within-format gradient NOT preserved; insufficient data to disentangle format from\n          task-type effects.\n    figures_description:\n      - >\n        Stacked-bar variance decomposition across 4 benchmarks — the \"anti-gradient\" figure.\n        No systematic left-to-right ordering; bars show qualitatively different component\n        structures. GSM8K bar dominated by residual (gray, 43%); HellaSwag dominated by\n        item_id (blue, 44%); model×item (orange) varies 3× across benchmarks. Horizontal\n        dashed line at item_id=42% (MMLU reference) shows other benchmarks scatter above\n        and below without trend.\n      - >\n        D-study projection curves per benchmark (G vs item count, 4 panels or 4 lines on\n        one panel). Curves have wildly different slopes and asymptotes. GSM8K curve is\n        nearly flat — even 1000 items only reaches G≈0.75 because residual dominates.\n        HellaSwag curve rises steeply, crossing G=0.80 at ~280 items.\n    analysis: >\n      The cross-benchmark gradient — the planned headline finding — does not materialize.\n      Item_id% varies only 8.7pp across benchmarks (35-44%), with no monotonic ordering by\n      task complexity, format, or any other interpretable dimension. The permutation test for\n      monotonicity yields p=0.41 (null). Model×item interaction varies 23pp across benchmarks\n      but in an unpredictable pattern (highest on GSM8K, lowest on HellaSwag).\n\n      However, the \"benchmark-specific variance fingerprints\" finding is itself publishable.\n      The fact that variance structure is irreducibly benchmark-specific — not predictable\n      from task taxonomy — means (a) benchmark-specific G-studies are mandatory, not optional,\n      (b) reproducibility budgets cannot be transferred across benchmarks, and (c) conclusions\n      about \"which factors matter\" from single-benchmark studies do not generalize. This is\n      arguably more surprising and practically important than a clean gradient.\n\n      GSM8K's pathological residual (43.2%) is a standalone finding: free-form generation\n      introduces irreducible stochasticity that makes G-theory decomposition qualitatively\n      different from MC benchmarks. The seed×item negative REML estimate and convergence\n      oscillation on HellaSwag precision×item suggest that Henderson I (designed for balanced\n      continuous data) struggles with certain benchmark-specific variance structures,\n      strengthening the case for benchmark-specific methodology.\n\n      Phase 2-4 blockage limits the analysis: no MATH benchmark prevents within-free-form\n      gradient testing, and only 4 models prevents the planned expansion to 6-8 models that\n      would strengthen the model-as-facet exchangeability argument. The paper must explicitly\n      scope to \"4 models × 4 benchmarks, exploratory Phase 1 analysis.\"\n\n  - name: \"Multi-hardware G-study\"\n    plan_id: \"exp-003\"\n    description: >\n      Completely blocked. No second GPU type becomes available during the experimental window.\n      Hardware claim remains scoped to \"numerical precision on RTX PRO 6000 (Blackwell).\"\n    expected_results:\n      status: blocked\n      n_gpu_types: 1\n      data_collected: 0\n      hardware_crossing_completed: false\n      reason: \"No alternative GPU allocation (A100/H100/AMD) secured\"\n    result_rationale: >\n      The experiment requires a fundamentally different GPU architecture to test hardware×methodology\n      interaction. In the worst tier, the compute allocation is fully consumed by exp-002 Phase 1\n      on the single available machine (westd-32570). The planned second machine (westd-15695) is\n      either unavailable, has the same GPU type, or has allocation conflicts. Without a second GPU\n      type, the entire experiment is infeasible.\n\n      Impact on paper: Claim 2 must be scoped to \"precision separability on a single GPU\n      architecture\" rather than \"infrastructure separability.\" This is already the planned\n      defensive scoping per D003/D007. The paper acknowledges hardware crossing as future work\n      and cites Yuan et al. (2025) for evidence that hardware effects exist but are unquantified\n      in a G-theory framework. This weakens but does not kill Claim 2 — precision separability\n      under binary scoring is still a valid confirmatory finding.\n    tables: []\n    figures_description: []\n    analysis: >\n      No analysis possible. Experiment contributes zero data. Paper must explicitly state\n      \"hardware limited to a single GPU architecture (NVIDIA RTX PRO 6000, Blackwell)\" in\n      Methods and Limitations. The separability claim (Claim 2) survives but is narrowly\n      scoped — \"precision × methodology separability\" rather than \"infrastructure × methodology\n      separability.\" Reviewer concern #1 (\"Only N GPU types\") is unaddressed and must be\n      handled as a stated limitation with future work framing.\n\n  - name: \"Comprehensive analysis\"\n    plan_id: \"exp-004\"\n    description: >\n      Analysis proceeds on available data: exp-001 MMLU (345,600 records, 4 models, full\n      design) + exp-002 Phase 1 (460,800 records, 4 models × 4 benchmarks, reduced design).\n      Most sub-analyses (a)-(q) are feasible on MMLU. Cross-benchmark sub-analyses are\n      limited by exp-002's reduced scope and pathological variance structures.\n    expected_results:\n      # --- (a) Model-as-facet reanalysis ---\n      model_as_facet_MMLU_item_id_pct: 42.1  # already validated in exp-001\n      model_as_facet_MMLU_model_x_item_pct: 33.6\n      model_as_facet_other_benchmarks: \"attempted but unreliable on GSM8K (residual >40% absorbs signal)\"\n      # --- (b) Bootstrap CIs ---\n      bootstrap_n_resamples: 1000\n      bootstrap_CI_item_id_MMLU: [36.0, 48.8]  # 95% CI\n      bootstrap_CI_model_x_item_MMLU: [28.4, 37.9]\n      bootstrap_CI_width_item_id_MMLU_pp: 12.8\n      bootstrap_CI_cross_benchmark: \"wide — ARC item_id CI [28, 47], GSM8K item_id CI [24, 48]\"\n      bootstrap_CI_GSM8K_residual: [35.1, 52.8]  # wide due to stochastic generation\n      # --- (c) D-study projections with CIs ---\n      D_study_MMLU_items_G80_CI: [430, 610]  # point estimate 510\n      D_study_MMLU_items_G90_CI: [1050, 1680]\n      D_study_cross_benchmark_CIs: \"too wide for practical guidance on GSM8K (G80: [600, 2100])\"\n      # --- (d) REML vs Henderson I ---\n      REML_Henderson_agreement_MMLU: \"all components within <0.5pp\"\n      REML_Henderson_agreement_ARC: \"within <1.2pp for top-3 components\"\n      REML_Henderson_agreement_GSM8K: \"diverge — Henderson I seed×item=1.8%, REML=-0.3% (clamped to 0)\"\n      REML_Henderson_agreement_HellaSwag: \"within <0.8pp except precision×item (Henderson 0.02%, REML oscillates 0.00-0.15%)\"\n      # --- (e) Logprob scoring ---\n      logprob_analysis_feasible: \"MMLU and ARC only (MC format with logprob access)\"\n      logprob_item_id_pct_MMLU: 55.3  # higher on continuous scale\n      logprob_model_x_item_pct_MMLU: 28.1\n      logprob_residual_pct_MMLU: 11.2  # lower residual on continuous scale\n      logprob_HellaSwag: \"feasible in principle (completion scoring) but not implemented\"\n      logprob_GSM8K: \"not feasible — free-form generation has no natural logprob score\"\n      # --- (f) Ranking stability ---\n      model_ranking_stable_across_precision_MMLU: true\n      model_ranking_stable_across_precision_GSM8K: false  # Mistral and Qwen swap at T=0.7\n      # --- (g) Higher-order interactions ---\n      3way_interactions_total_MMLU_pct: 4.2\n      3way_interactions_total_ARC_pct: 6.8\n      3way_interactions_total_GSM8K_pct: 2.1  # small because residual absorbs everything\n      residual_after_3way_MMLU_pct: 15.3\n      # --- (h) IRT vs G-theory comparison ---\n      IRT_items_G80_MMLU: 116\n      Gtheory_items_G80_MMLU: 510\n      IRT_to_Gtheory_ratio: 4.4\n      IRT_comparison_feasible_on: \"MMLU only — other benchmarks lack sufficient data or have pathological structures\"\n      # --- (i) Power analysis ---\n      power_to_detect_5pct_interaction_MMLU: 0.99\n      power_to_detect_5pct_interaction_ARC: 0.94\n      power_to_detect_5pct_interaction_GSM8K: 0.78  # reduced by high residual\n      # --- (k) Fixed vs random sensitivity ---\n      fixed_vs_random_delta_MMLU_pp: 2.8  # <5%, robust\n      fixed_vs_random_delta_GSM8K_pp: 8.4  # >5%, NOT robust — temperature dominates GSM8K\n      # --- (l) Binary data limitation ---\n      bernoulli_p1p_correlation_with_item_var_MMLU: 0.31  # moderate\n      bernoulli_p1p_correlation_with_item_var_GSM8K: 0.68  # high — low accuracy items have compressed variance\n      # --- (m) Bootstrap vs jackknife ---\n      bootstrap_jackknife_CI_ratio_MMLU: 0.98  # excellent agreement\n      bootstrap_jackknife_CI_ratio_ARC: 1.03\n      bootstrap_jackknife_CI_ratio_GSM8K: 1.14  # jackknife wider, suggesting bootstrap anti-conservative\n      # --- (n) Design asymmetry ---\n      MMLU_full_vs_matched_2x2_delta_item_id_pp: 2.9  # <5%, robust\n      MMLU_full_vs_matched_2x2_delta_model_x_item_pp: 1.4\n      # --- (o) G at full benchmark size ---\n      G_at_14K_items_MMLU_projection: 0.987  # full MMLU is highly reliable\n      G_at_full_ARC_2590_items_projection: 0.953\n      G_at_full_GSM8K_1319_items_projection: 0.812  # barely crosses 0.80 even at full size\n      # --- (p) Logprob across benchmarks ---\n      logprob_cross_benchmark: \"only MMLU and ARC feasible, insufficient for cross-benchmark claims\"\n      # --- (q) GPU cost analysis ---\n      compute_cost_per_G80_MMLU: \"~$45 (510 items × 4 models × 6 prompts × 6 seeds)\"\n      compute_cost_per_G80_GSM8K: \"~$210 (1150 items × 4 models × 6 prompts × 6 seeds, longer generation)\"\n    result_rationale: >\n      MMLU analyses are strong and largely replicate exp-001 findings with bootstrap CIs\n      and additional robustness checks. The IRT vs G-theory comparison (4.4× item ratio)\n      is clean on MMLU and becomes a primary contribution.\n\n      Cross-benchmark analyses are degraded by three compounding factors:\n      (1) Only 4 benchmarks (no MATH) with only 4 models — limited statistical power for\n      cross-benchmark generalizations and wide bootstrap CIs.\n      (2) GSM8K's pathological variance structure (residual >40%, negative REML estimates)\n      makes several analyses unreliable or uninterpretable on that benchmark. The high\n      residual means G-theory components are estimated with low precision, widening CIs\n      and making D-study projections impractical (CI for G80 items: [600, 2100]).\n      (3) Fixed-vs-random sensitivity analysis fails on GSM8K (Δ>5pp), meaning the\n      methodological choice of treating temperature as random vs fixed materially affects\n      results — another benchmark-specific pathology.\n\n      REML diverges from Henderson I on GSM8K because the balanced design assumption is\n      stressed by near-zero or negative components. Henderson I is unbiased for balanced\n      designs but produces interpretable results even when components are near-zero; REML\n      tries to find ML estimates and can produce negative values that must be clamped.\n      This divergence is itself a finding: Henderson I is reliable for well-behaved MC\n      benchmarks but may be problematic for free-form generation benchmarks.\n\n      The logprob analysis cannot extend beyond MC formats (MMLU, ARC) because free-form\n      and completion tasks lack a natural single-token probability score. This means the\n      \"Evaluation Metrics\" claim in the title is only partially supported, and the title\n      may need adjustment per MD9.\n\n      Bootstrap vs jackknife agreement is good on MMLU/ARC (ratio 0.98-1.03) but marginal\n      on GSM8K (ratio 1.14), suggesting bootstrap CIs on GSM8K are slightly anti-conservative\n      due to non-independence in the stochastic generation process.\n    tables:\n      - name: \"Table: Bootstrap 95% CIs for All Variance Components (MMLU, 4 models)\"\n        description: >\n          7 variance components × point estimate + 95% CI. item_id: 42.1% [36.0, 48.8],\n          model×item: 33.6% [28.4, 37.9], residual: 19.5% [16.2, 23.1]. All CIs exclude\n          zero for top-4 components. prompt×item and seed×item CIs overlap.\n      - name: \"Table: REML vs Henderson I Comparison Across Benchmarks\"\n        description: >\n          4 benchmarks × 7 components × Henderson I estimate + REML estimate + Δ.\n          MMLU: all Δ < 0.5pp. ARC: Δ < 1.2pp. HellaSwag: precision×item oscillates.\n          GSM8K: seed×item diverges (1.8% vs clamped 0%). Conclusion: Henderson I reliable\n          for MC benchmarks; caution needed for free-form generation.\n      - name: \"Table: IRT vs G-Theory Item Requirements (MMLU)\"\n        description: >\n          Reliability threshold × IRT items × G-theory items × ratio. G≥0.70: 55 vs 210\n          (3.8×). G≥0.80: 116 vs 510 (4.4×). G≥0.90: 280 vs 1350 (4.8×). Decomposition\n          of G-theory excess: prompt×item accounts for 22%, seed×item 23%, residual 55%\n          of additional items.\n      - name: \"Table: Fixed vs Random Sensitivity Analysis\"\n        description: >\n          4 benchmarks × top-4 components × random estimate + fixed estimate + Δ.\n          MMLU Δ < 3pp (robust). GSM8K Δ = 8.4pp for item_id (NOT robust — temperature\n          reclassification redistributes 8pp from temperature to residual).\n      - name: \"Table: D-Study Budget Comparison with Bootstrap CIs\"\n        description: >\n          4 benchmarks × items for G≥0.80 [95% CI]. MMLU: 510 [430, 610]. ARC: 340 [260, 470].\n          HellaSwag: 280 [210, 380]. GSM8K: 1150 [600, 2100]. The GSM8K CI spans 3.5×,\n          making the point estimate practically meaningless.\n    figures_description:\n      - >\n        Forest plot of variance component CIs across 4 benchmarks. Each benchmark is a\n        panel; within each panel, components are rows with point estimates (dots) and 95%\n        CI whiskers. Visually shows that CI widths vary dramatically across benchmarks\n        (tight on MMLU, wide on GSM8K) and component rankings are inconsistent.\n      - >\n        IRT vs G-theory comparison bar chart (MMLU only). Two bars per reliability threshold\n        (0.70, 0.80, 0.90). G-theory bar decomposed into sub-segments showing which\n        facets (prompt×item, seed×item, residual) account for the extra items beyond IRT.\n        Clear visual showing the \"multi-facet reliability cost.\"\n      - >\n        D-study projection curves with 95% CI ribbons for 4 benchmarks (overlaid on single\n        panel). MMLU and HellaSwag curves rise steeply with narrow ribbons. GSM8K curve\n        is nearly flat with a wide ribbon that barely touches G=0.80 at n=1150.\n    analysis: >\n      exp-004 is the analytical core of the paper under worst tier. The MMLU-specific\n      analyses are strong: bootstrap CIs confirm top-3 component ordering, IRT comparison\n      produces the clean 4.4× ratio, REML validates Henderson I, and the binary data\n      robustness checks all pass. These form the backbone of the paper.\n\n      Cross-benchmark analyses reveal the \"irreducibly benchmark-specific\" finding but\n      cannot support strong quantitative claims due to wide CIs and benchmark-specific\n      pathologies. GSM8K is particularly problematic: high residual → wide CIs → unreliable\n      D-study projections → fixed-vs-random sensitivity failure. The paper should present\n      GSM8K results as \"illustrative of pathological variance structure in free-form\n      generation\" rather than as reliable G-theory estimates.\n\n      The IRT vs G-theory comparison is limited to MMLU (the only benchmark with full\n      design + clean variance structure + IRT comparability). This is sufficient for a\n      strong numbered contribution but prevents claiming \"cross-benchmark IRT comparison.\"\n\n      Key paper structure implication: MMLU analysis (exp-001 + exp-004 MMLU-specific)\n      occupies 60-70% of the results section. Cross-benchmark analysis (exp-002 + exp-004\n      cross-benchmark) occupies 30-40% as an \"exploratory extension\" with explicit\n      caveats about reduced design, limited benchmarks, and pathological structures.\n\n  - name: \"14B model extension\"\n    plan_id: \"exp-005\"\n    description: >\n      Blocked. Depends on exp-003 (hardware) which is blocked, and requires ≥48GB VRAM\n      GPUs not currently allocated. No 14B model data is collected.\n    expected_results:\n      status: blocked\n      n_14B_models_tested: 0\n      scale_analysis_completed: false\n      reason: \"Depends on exp-003 (blocked); requires high-VRAM GPU not available\"\n    result_rationale: >\n      exp-005 has two blocking dependencies: (1) it was designed to run on datacenter GPUs\n      at BF16/FP16 after exp-003 establishes the multi-hardware baseline, and (2) 14B models\n      require ≥48GB VRAM for comfortable inference with vLLM, which is available on the\n      existing RTX PRO 6000 (96GB) but the GPU budget is fully consumed by exp-002 Phase 1.\n      Even if the hardware dependency were relaxed, the compute hours (100h) compete directly\n      with exp-002's needs. Under worst-tier resource constraints, P1 experiments are\n      systematically deprioritized in favor of P0 experiments.\n\n      Impact on paper: The \"model scale\" dimension is entirely absent. The paper must state\n      \"all models are 7-9B parameter scale\" as a limitation. This is defensible — the paper\n      studies variance structure, not scaling laws — but reviewers may flag it. The planned\n      comparison between dense (Qwen3-14B) and reasoning-distilled (DeepSeek-R1-Distill-14B)\n      architectures is lost. This comparison would have been interesting for the model×item\n      interaction analysis (does distillation change the interaction pattern?) but is not\n      essential for the core claims.\n    tables: []\n    figures_description: []\n    analysis: >\n      No analysis possible. Limitation statement: \"Our analysis is limited to 7-9B parameter\n      open-weight models. Whether variance structure changes with model scale is an important\n      open question — larger models may show reduced model×item interaction if they converge\n      toward similar capability profiles, or increased interaction if scale enables more\n      specialized item-level behaviors.\"\n\n  - name: \"Fine-tuning G-study\"\n    plan_id: \"exp-006\"\n    description: >\n      Blocked by compute reallocation. The 1,728 fine-tuning runs (432 per task × 4 tasks)\n      require ~200 GPU hours on westd-32570, which is fully allocated to exp-002 Phase 1\n      and exp-001 post-processing. Even partial completion (1 task) would consume 50+ hours.\n    expected_results:\n      status: blocked\n      n_finetuning_runs_completed: 0\n      tasks_completed: 0\n      reason: \"GPU budget fully consumed by P0 experiments (exp-002 Phase 1)\"\n    result_rationale: >\n      exp-006 is independent (no experimental dependencies) but competes for the same GPU\n      resource. Under worst-tier constraints, exp-002 Phase 1 alone consumes 200-250 GPU\n      hours (4 models × 4 benchmarks × ~15h per model-benchmark combination with the full\n      prompt/seed/ordering crossing). With exp-001 post-processing reruns and validation\n      checks, the total GPU budget is exhausted before exp-006 can start.\n\n      Even if exp-006 were partially completed (e.g., SST-2 only), the results would be\n      insufficient for even a Discussion-section preliminary observation — a single\n      fine-tuning task cannot support variance structure comparison between inference and\n      fine-tuning paradigms.\n\n      Impact on paper: Fine-tuning analysis is mentioned as future work in Discussion. The\n      paper explicitly qualifies: \"Our analysis addresses inference-time evaluation only.\n      Fine-tuning variance structure — where learning rate, data order, and seed interact\n      with task-specific loss landscapes — is a qualitatively different problem that we\n      leave for future work.\" Per D006 Dream #3 directive, fine-tuning was already downgraded\n      to Discussion-section preliminary observation, so blockage has minimal impact on paper\n      structure.\n    tables: []\n    figures_description: []\n    analysis: >\n      No analysis possible. The fine-tuning extension is entirely absent from the paper.\n      Per existing framing directives, this was already downgraded to Discussion section.\n      Cite Bui et al. (2025) for evidence that fine-tuning variance structure differs\n      substantially (RTES variance=18.22 for RoBERTa on COPA) and position as motivated\n      future work.\n\n  - name: \"LayerCast precision study\"\n    plan_id: \"exp-007\"\n    description: >\n      Deprioritized (P2). Not started due to GPU budget exhaustion from P0 experiments.\n      LayerCast (FP16 storage + FP32 compute) as a variance mitigation strategy is not\n      tested.\n    expected_results:\n      status: blocked\n      layercast_tested: false\n      variance_reduction_quantified: false\n      reason: \"P2 priority; GPU budget exhausted by P0 experiments\"\n    result_rationale: >\n      exp-007 is the lowest-priority experiment (P2) and depends on exp-001 being complete\n      (it is). However, the 30 GPU hours required compete with exp-002 Phase 1 needs. Under\n      worst-tier constraints, all discretionary GPU budget is consumed by the P0 pipeline.\n      Additionally, the scientific value of LayerCast testing is reduced once BF16-vs-FP16\n      sensitivity analysis (already completed in exp-001) shows BF16≈FP16 (Δ=0.23pp) — if\n      standard precision choices don't matter, testing a mixed-precision strategy is low\n      priority.\n\n      Impact on paper: No variance mitigation strategy is tested. The paper's D-study\n      budgets address \"how many items/prompts/seeds\" but not \"how to reduce precision\n      variance.\" This is consistent with the finding that precision contributes <0.1%\n      variance under binary scoring — there is nothing to mitigate. The paper can note:\n      \"LayerCast and other mixed-precision strategies may be relevant for text-exact-match\n      scoring where precision×temperature interaction is detectable (1.3%).\"\n    tables: []\n    figures_description: []\n    analysis: >\n      No analysis possible. Minimal impact on paper — precision is already shown to be\n      negligible under binary scoring. LayerCast is mentioned as a potential mitigation\n      for text-exact-match scoring in the Discussion section.\n\n  - name: \"MATH benchmark (merged into exp-002 Phase 2)\"\n    plan_id: \"exp-008\"\n    description: >\n      Blocked. MATH was merged into exp-002 as the 5th benchmark in Phase 2, which is\n      blocked by answer extraction pipeline issues. Free-form symbolic math answer\n      normalization (handling equivalent forms: \"1/2\", \"0.5\", \"\\\\frac{1}{2}\", \"0.500\")\n      requires engineering work that stalls.\n    expected_results:\n      status: blocked\n      math_items_collected: 0\n      answer_extraction_pipeline_status: \"incomplete — symbolic normalization handles ~70% of MATH answers correctly, remaining 30% require regex/sympy extensions\"\n      reason: \"Merged into exp-002 Phase 2; blocked by answer normalization engineering\"\n    result_rationale: >\n      MATH benchmark presents unique data engineering challenges compared to MC benchmarks\n      (MMLU, ARC, HellaSwag) and even compared to GSM8K (which has simpler numerical\n      answers). MATH answers include symbolic expressions, fractions, roots, and multiple\n      equivalent forms that require robust normalization. The extraction pipeline handles\n      simple numerical answers (GSM8K-style) but fails on ~30% of MATH items where the\n      answer is symbolic. Building a robust sympy-based normalization pipeline is estimated\n      at 20-30 engineering hours — work that is deprioritized under worst-tier constraints\n      where the focus is on completing Phase 1 analysis and writing.\n\n      Impact on paper: The absence of MATH critically undermines the within-format gradient\n      test. With only GSM8K as a free-form benchmark, the within-format gradient analysis\n      has no free-form comparator. The paper cannot distinguish whether GSM8K's low item_id%\n      (35.4%) is due to free-form format, mathematical reasoning task type, or\n      stochastic generation. This ambiguity is a significant limitation for the\n      \"benchmark-specific fingerprints\" framing — without MATH, the paper cannot determine\n      whether the fingerprint is driven by format or task content within the free-form category.\n\n      The paper addresses this by: (a) acknowledging the limitation explicitly, (b) noting\n      that MATH's absence prevents within-free-form gradient testing, and (c) framing MATH\n      as the highest-priority extension for future work. The \"benchmark-specific fingerprints\"\n      claim is still supported by the MC benchmarks (MMLU vs ARC have different structures\n      despite same format) and the cross-format comparison (MC vs completion vs free-form),\n      but the within-format argument is weaker.\n    tables: []\n    figures_description: []\n    analysis: >\n      No analysis possible. The paper's cross-benchmark analysis is limited to 4 benchmarks\n      (MMLU, ARC, HellaSwag, GSM8K) spanning 3 formats (MC, completion, free-form) but\n      with only 1-2 benchmarks per format. Within-format gradient testing is limited to\n      MC (MMLU vs ARC = 2 data points). The \"benchmark-specific fingerprints\" finding\n      survives but is less compelling without a within-format comparator for free-form tasks.\n```","stop_reason":"end_turn","session_id":"4b24b73a-f9a4-4c38-9191-0dbd0db6438e","total_cost_usd":0.38068900000000006,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":0,"cache_read_input_tokens":30536,"output_tokens":14066,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":14066,"cache_read_input_tokens":30536,"cache_creation_input_tokens":0,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"},"modelUsage":{"claude-haiku-4-5-20251001":{"inputTokens":13666,"outputTokens":19,"cacheReadInputTokens":0,"cacheCreationInputTokens":0,"webSearchRequests":0,"costUSD":0.013760999999999999,"contextWindow":200000,"ma